WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.223 --> 00:00:03.290
(chill guitar music)

00:00:03.290 --> 00:00:05.960
- [Wes] On a research boat,
usually it's the new guy

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:07.363
who gets the dirtiest job.

00:00:08.650 --> 00:00:09.920
- [Man] You guys got spit on.

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:11.893
- This time is no exception.

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:18.210
I'm Wes Larson, a wildlife biologist

00:00:18.210 --> 00:00:21.460
and lover of the outdoors for
as long as I can remember.

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:22.840
I'm traveling the country to find

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:25.100
the next generation of conservationists.

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:26.250
The people on the front lines

00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:28.030
of the fight to understand our planet,

00:00:28.030 --> 00:00:30.080
and protect the animals we share it with.

00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:38.647
We got a pretty big hermit crab.

00:00:38.647 --> 00:00:40.830
(laughing)

00:00:40.830 --> 00:00:42.610
Today, I'm headed to Sarasota,

00:00:42.610 --> 00:00:44.420
on the Gulf Coast of Florida.

00:00:44.420 --> 00:00:47.360
It's home to a beautiful but
little understood animal,

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:48.613
the spotted eagle ray.

00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:52.010
I'm joining up with a research team

00:00:52.010 --> 00:00:55.560
from Florida Atlantic
University and Mote Marine Lab.

00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:57.420
They're some of the first
scientists in the world

00:00:57.420 --> 00:00:59.273
to study this animal in depth.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:02.720
So, Breanna, what are we doing today?

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:04.190
- So, today we're gonna attempt to

00:01:04.190 --> 00:01:06.260
visually spot some spotted eagle rays.

00:01:06.260 --> 00:01:08.710
Bring it onboard, take a bunch of samples,

00:01:08.710 --> 00:01:10.740
and then we'll release
it back into the wild.

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:12.280
- I am way excited.

00:01:14.368 --> 00:01:17.130
Breanna DeGroot is a
master student at FAU.

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:18.500
She's been obsessed with rays

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:20.660
ever since she was 11 years old.

00:01:20.660 --> 00:01:23.180
And, she's leading the
field research today.

00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:25.250
Spotted eagle rays are
pretty understudied,

00:01:25.250 --> 00:01:27.400
we really don't know a
lot about them, right?

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:29.810
- Yeah, there's not a
whole lot of information

00:01:29.810 --> 00:01:33.130
about just even their
basic biology or ecology.

00:01:33.130 --> 00:01:35.210
So, what do they mean for the environment,

00:01:35.210 --> 00:01:36.960
how are they affecting the environment,

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:38.630
how many of 'em are out there?

00:01:38.630 --> 00:01:40.920
And, from an environmental component,

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:42.700
they're really important to keeping

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:44.060
the ecosystem in balance.

00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:45.910
So, it's really important
that we understand

00:01:45.910 --> 00:01:47.360
basic questions about them,

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:49.260
which is what we're trying to do here.

00:01:53.682 --> 00:01:55.360
- So, we're up on the spotting tower.

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:56.210
It's quite the view up here.

00:01:56.210 --> 00:01:57.650
You can definitely see into the water

00:01:57.650 --> 00:01:59.710
a lot better than you can down there.

00:01:59.710 --> 00:02:01.293
- They stand out pretty good.

00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:04.890
We're gonna go over a grass bed right now.

00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:06.830
They have a tendency to hide in the grass.

00:02:06.830 --> 00:02:08.560
- [Wes] What we're looking
for is a ray large enough

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:10.540
to surgically implant a tracker.

00:02:10.540 --> 00:02:12.853
And, it takes an
experienced eye to spot one.

00:02:13.710 --> 00:02:14.574
- Right there.

00:02:14.574 --> 00:02:15.490
- [Woman] Is it an eagle ray?

00:02:15.490 --> 00:02:17.440
- Yeah, I believe so.

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:18.720
That's him, that's him.

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:19.660
That's an eagle ray, dude.

00:02:19.660 --> 00:02:20.989
- [Man] Be ready.

00:02:20.989 --> 00:02:23.156
(yelling)

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:26.760
- Catching a ray is a
complicated maneuver.

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:28.760
We have to completely
encircle it with the net,

00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:32.030
or the ray will escape, and
you've gotta be quick about it.

00:02:32.030 --> 00:02:34.140
The water's really choppy,
and these maneuvers

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:36.333
that they do with the boat
are really aggressive.

00:02:37.653 --> 00:02:38.933
- [Woman] He's in the net.

00:02:38.933 --> 00:02:39.782
- [Man] You got him?

00:02:39.782 --> 00:02:41.132
Yeah, he's in there.

00:02:41.132 --> 00:02:42.799
- We got him.
- Yeah.

00:02:45.610 --> 00:02:47.010
- [Wes] Once we've got it surrounded,

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:48.883
we need to bring in the ray by hand.

00:02:50.730 --> 00:02:52.933
- He's in the middle;
he's going to the north.

00:02:58.170 --> 00:02:59.943
Oh, geez.
- Beauty.

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:04.710
- This ray turns out to
be tiny, just a juvenile.

00:03:04.710 --> 00:03:06.170
My job is to hold it in place

00:03:06.170 --> 00:03:07.720
while the team does their work.

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:12.230
And, despite its size, it's not easy.

00:03:12.230 --> 00:03:15.104
Yeah, I can see how a big
one would be pretty strong.

00:03:15.104 --> 00:03:16.162
- So, flip him.

00:03:16.162 --> 00:03:16.995
- Flip him?

00:03:16.995 --> 00:03:17.828
OK.

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:21.140
As soon as we flipped him over,

00:03:21.140 --> 00:03:23.750
you can really see how
much he's calmed down.

00:03:23.750 --> 00:03:26.300
That's a state that they go
into called tonic mobility,

00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:28.400
it's kinda like the ray's hypnotized.

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:31.950
A lot easier to work with
than when he was on his belly.

00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:33.950
They get, they're quite a
bit bigger than this, right?

00:03:33.950 --> 00:03:36.210
- [Breanna] Yes, they get
to be about seven feet.

00:03:36.210 --> 00:03:38.032
- I wanna catch a seven foot ray today.

00:03:38.032 --> 00:03:40.500
- We'll look for a seven foot ray.

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:41.610
- [Wes] This ray is too small

00:03:41.610 --> 00:03:43.600
to surgically implant a tracking device.

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:45.690
So, once we've gathered what data we can,

00:03:45.690 --> 00:03:47.459
it's time to let him go.

00:03:47.459 --> 00:03:48.873
He's off into the wild.

00:03:48.873 --> 00:03:50.440
And, while the spotters keep an eye out

00:03:50.440 --> 00:03:53.573
for a big ray, we take
a break from the hunt.

00:03:55.130 --> 00:03:56.860
When you begin researching an animal,

00:03:56.860 --> 00:03:59.360
one of the most fundamental
things to understand

00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:00.900
is its eating habits.

00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:02.530
And, to figure out the eagle ray's,

00:04:02.530 --> 00:04:04.498
the team has come up
with a creative solution.

00:04:04.498 --> 00:04:06.219
- [Woman] We're going in, everybody in.

00:04:06.219 --> 00:04:07.469
Scour the area.

00:04:12.767 --> 00:04:15.310
- So, Breanna, this is kinda
what we're looking for, right?

00:04:15.310 --> 00:04:17.369
- [Breanna] That's exactly
what we're looking for.

00:04:17.369 --> 00:04:18.620
- [Wes] And, this is a gastropod?

00:04:18.620 --> 00:04:19.453
- Yes, so.

00:04:19.453 --> 00:04:20.286
- Or, for the layman.

00:04:20.286 --> 00:04:21.119
- Fancy name for a snail.
- A snail.

00:04:21.119 --> 00:04:23.750
K.
- Yeah, a marine snail.

00:04:23.750 --> 00:04:26.290
- [Wes] We take back the
snails and clams to the lab,

00:04:26.290 --> 00:04:29.070
where the team has two wild
rays in a holding tank.

00:04:29.070 --> 00:04:31.550
- We're gonna put some
prey items into the water,

00:04:31.550 --> 00:04:33.880
and we're going to record the sounds

00:04:33.880 --> 00:04:36.250
that they're making while
they're crunching down

00:04:36.250 --> 00:04:37.940
on the hard shelled prey items.

00:04:37.940 --> 00:04:40.130
And, we're trying to decipher if

00:04:40.130 --> 00:04:41.740
these different prey items are making

00:04:41.740 --> 00:04:44.330
different signature sounds.

00:04:44.330 --> 00:04:46.500
- [Wes] By tracking the sounds
that different shells make

00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:48.590
when the rays crunch
them, the team believes

00:04:48.590 --> 00:04:50.977
that they can decipher
the eagle ray's diet.

00:04:50.977 --> 00:04:53.350
(crunching)

00:04:53.350 --> 00:04:55.300
- And, that's the big.
- He just crunched it there.

00:04:55.300 --> 00:04:56.390
- [Breanna] Exactly, yup.

00:04:56.390 --> 00:04:58.970
Well, the hope is to put
these listening stations

00:04:58.970 --> 00:05:00.750
out into the environment, and we can know

00:05:00.750 --> 00:05:03.210
exactly what they're eating,
when they're eating it.

00:05:03.210 --> 00:05:05.023
And, how much they're consuming.

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:08.920
- [Wes] As the captive rays crunch away,

00:05:08.920 --> 00:05:11.110
we get word that another
has been spotted offshore.

00:05:11.110 --> 00:05:11.943
- That's bigger.

00:05:11.943 --> 00:05:13.760
- [Wes] So, it's time to
head back into the gulf,

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:15.760
for one last attempt to catch a big one.

00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:18.840
- We just got word that there's another

00:05:18.840 --> 00:05:20.790
spotted eagle ray about double the size

00:05:20.790 --> 00:05:22.730
of the ones that we've
currently been catching.

00:05:22.730 --> 00:05:25.750
So, we're on our way to go relocate it,

00:05:25.750 --> 00:05:28.573
and hopefully, we'll
get another ray onboard.

00:05:28.573 --> 00:05:29.481
- [Man] Get ready.

00:05:29.481 --> 00:05:30.897
- Ready?
- Ready?

00:05:32.190 --> 00:05:34.940
- [Wes] It's pretty clear when
the team grabs the other net

00:05:34.940 --> 00:05:37.080
that this is a much bigger ray.

00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:38.970
- [Woman] To your left, to your left.

00:05:38.970 --> 00:05:40.387
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:05:41.952 --> 00:05:44.109
- [Man] Yeah, high.

00:05:44.109 --> 00:05:44.942
- You guys ready?

00:05:44.942 --> 00:05:45.775
- [Man] Nice and easy.

00:05:45.775 --> 00:05:46.833
You got enough water yet?

00:05:47.940 --> 00:05:49.956
Lift him all the way up.
- All the way up.

00:05:49.956 --> 00:05:51.206
All the way up.

00:05:53.162 --> 00:05:55.513
- Lower down.
- Down, down, down.

00:05:55.513 --> 00:05:56.346
Perfect.

00:05:58.720 --> 00:05:59.922
- [Man] Little bigger
than the last one, huh?

00:05:59.922 --> 00:06:00.755
- Yeah. (laughing)

00:06:00.755 --> 00:06:02.383
- Yeah, just a little bit.

00:06:02.383 --> 00:06:05.420
It's unreal how strong their wings are.

00:06:05.420 --> 00:06:08.390
This ray is over 5 feet wide
from wingtip to wingtip.

00:06:08.390 --> 00:06:11.220
Plenty big enough for the
surgery Breanna is hoping to do.

00:06:11.220 --> 00:06:12.621
Turn around like that?

00:06:12.621 --> 00:06:13.454
- Yeah.

00:06:13.454 --> 00:06:15.600
- [Wes] And, my job,
holding the ray upside down

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:18.790
and perfectly still while she
implants the tracking tag.

00:06:18.790 --> 00:06:21.210
- So, what you want is
to like, yeah, like lay.

00:06:21.210 --> 00:06:22.650
Figure out the best position for you

00:06:22.650 --> 00:06:24.693
to raise this part with your body.

00:06:24.693 --> 00:06:26.340
- OK.

00:06:26.340 --> 00:06:28.950
- So, preparing all of
our surgery supplies

00:06:28.950 --> 00:06:31.580
to do an internal acoustic surgery.

00:06:31.580 --> 00:06:33.430
It's a really quick procedure,

00:06:33.430 --> 00:06:37.430
where an internal tag is
implanted into their belly area.

00:06:37.430 --> 00:06:39.660
And, it allows up to
follow their movements

00:06:39.660 --> 00:06:42.250
as they travel up and down the coastline.

00:06:42.250 --> 00:06:44.400
- Is she gonna react to this?

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:45.233
- She shouldn't.

00:06:46.120 --> 00:06:46.995
Ready?

00:06:46.995 --> 00:06:48.680
- [Woman] Yeah.

00:06:48.680 --> 00:06:50.900
- [Breanna] If you start
to feel her clench.

00:06:50.900 --> 00:06:51.733
- K.
- Just let me know.

00:06:51.733 --> 00:06:52.566
- I will.

00:06:53.740 --> 00:06:57.090
Sharks and rays don't feel
pain the same way humans do.

00:06:57.090 --> 00:06:58.250
And, with the ray on its back,

00:06:58.250 --> 00:07:00.893
it stays calm and relaxed
throughout the procedure.

00:07:02.450 --> 00:07:03.621
Yeah, that was quick.

00:07:03.621 --> 00:07:06.220
You guys did a good job.

00:07:06.220 --> 00:07:08.500
The surgery is over in just a few minutes.

00:07:08.500 --> 00:07:10.450
This tag will allow the team to track her

00:07:10.450 --> 00:07:13.350
for years to come, giving
them a detailed picture

00:07:13.350 --> 00:07:16.340
of where eagle rays live,
and how far they travel.

00:07:16.340 --> 00:07:17.590
Putting us one step closer

00:07:17.590 --> 00:07:19.540
to understanding this beautiful animal.

00:07:23.780 --> 00:07:25.780
I come from a mammal background.

00:07:25.780 --> 00:07:28.020
And, working with mammals,
there's kind of this

00:07:28.020 --> 00:07:30.210
natural connection that
you have with them.

00:07:30.210 --> 00:07:31.570
And, I didn't really expect to have

00:07:31.570 --> 00:07:33.130
that sort of connection with these rays.

00:07:33.130 --> 00:07:34.880
But, when we pulled 'em up onto the boat

00:07:34.880 --> 00:07:36.990
I felt this intelligence
coming out of them.

00:07:36.990 --> 00:07:38.790
They're a very personable animal.

00:07:38.790 --> 00:07:41.080
- Yeah, that's the
reaction most people have.

00:07:41.080 --> 00:07:43.693
They're highly intelligent,
and obviously, their beauty

00:07:43.693 --> 00:07:47.743
and grace just helps us form
another connection to them.

00:07:48.900 --> 00:07:50.690
- [Wes] I've heard people
say there isn't much mystery

00:07:50.690 --> 00:07:52.950
left in nature, but eagle rays remind me

00:07:52.950 --> 00:07:53.783
that there are still animals

00:07:53.783 --> 00:07:55.990
we know hardly anything about.

00:07:55.990 --> 00:07:57.730
Answering the basic questions can help us

00:07:57.730 --> 00:07:59.640
better understand them and, in turn,

00:07:59.640 --> 00:08:01.600
protect whole ecosystems.

00:08:01.600 --> 00:08:04.290
Watching this discovery
firsthand was a true privilege.

00:08:04.290 --> 00:08:06.150
It makes me wonder what
other hidden secrets

00:08:06.150 --> 00:08:07.450
must be waiting out there.

